          swi_d `show clock` 05:44:34 UTC ("Timezone: UTC, Timezone Offset: +00:00,
          Summer time zone: None"), box 05:44:48 UTC -- swi_d = box clock - 3 s, well
          inside the one-second resolution of a syslog stamp. Every timestamp quoted
          below is swi_d's own.)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2 = swi_d (DUT); Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     T33249 "IPv4_ARP - ARP Logging". No authored step text exists for this case,
          in AWPTCM or in ck.db: an exact-title lookup found step text for one other
          case in this campaign and none for this one, so there are no authored steps
          and no authored expected results. The headings below say what was actually
          exercised. The case was graded against:
            - X960 platform support TPS row 21 "ARP Logging", priority 1, "Yes", note
              column empty;
            - the IP Feature Overview and Configuration Guide, ARP logging section;
            - the DUT's own CLI surface, read off by a read-only `?` probe (quoted in
              STEP 0).
          Objective: with `arp log` configured the DUT logs ARP cache events caused by
          real ARP traffic; with it absent it logs none; the `mac-address-format ieee`
          sub-option changes how the MAC is rendered; both are removable; and the
          setting survives a reboot and still logs afterwards. 4 steps as executed,
          plus a baseline.
Build:    swi_d  sin_x96056-20261012-24  (flash:/x960-tb277.rel, build date Mon Oct 12 2026)
          AT-x960-56YSQ, S/N A2WQS700B, standalone, console u2 @115200
Configs:  swi_d.cfg (running-config after setup, before step 1; saved to startup: no)

VERDICT: PASS -- an identical crafted ARP resolution produces zero ARP_LOG lines with
the feature off and a correct log line per cache event with it on; the IEEE
mac-address-format sub-option changes the MAC rendering and nothing else; both forms
are removable, each proven in the log output that follows as well as in running-config;
and the setting survives a reboot and still logs.
      baseline, `arp log` absent ........... a complete ARP resolution on the wire,
                                             0 ARP_LOG lines in `show log`
      `arp log` enabled .................... the same resolution gives `del` + `add`,
                                             each with port, VLAN, MAC and IP
      line content matched to the capture .. port1.0.48 / vlan48 / 0011.2233.4402 /
                                             192.168.48.2, all four as sent
      facility and emitter ................. user.notice, HSL[936]
      `mac-address-format ieee` ............ the same event pair rendered
                                             00-11-22-33-44-02 instead of 0011.2233.4402
      held as its own config line .......... `arp log` and `arp log mac-address-format
                                             ieee` are two lines, not one
      removal of the sub-option ............ line gone, logging continues, MAC reverts
                                             to the AW+ dotted form
      removal of the feature ............... line gone, the identical leg gives 0
                                             ARP_LOG lines
      reboot ............................... `arp log` back, and a crafted resolution
                                             after the reboot still logs
      reboot was real ...................... the emitting process id changed, HSL[936]
                                             -> HSL[944]
CAVEAT:  Exercised with one dynamic entry on one VLAN, in both event directions (`del`
         from a cache clear and `add` from a resolution). `ieee` is the only value the
         CLI offers for `mac-address-format`, so the A/B covers the whole sub-option.
         The log was read with `show log` from the buffered log; no remote syslog
         destination was exercised, and no rate or volume behaviour was measured.

