            192.168.48.2 / 00:11:22:33:44:02 are scapy fictions.
  tools   : tools/arpleg.py on tb277 with `--probe`, `--watch`, `--answer` and
            `--answer-iface`; tools/cfg.py, tools/ckcon.py and tools/ckyn.py drive the
            console; tools/ckreload.py drove the reload; tools/rc2cfg.py saved
            swi_d.cfg; tools/qmark.py ran the read-only `?` probes.

  Method: every leg drives a COMPLETE, CRAFTED ARP resolution rather than inspecting a
  table -- arpleg.py sends routed probes that force the DUT to broadcast an ARP request,
  and its responder replies, so the DUT creates a genuine dynamic entry. `clear log
  buffered` runs immediately before each leg, so the log read afterwards holds that
  leg's events and nothing earlier, and `clear arp-cache` runs with it, so each leg
  produces a `del` (the clear) and an `add` (the re-learn). Counts below match the
  string `ARP_LOG`, not `arp`: an "arp" match over `show log` also catches the IMISH
  command-audit echo of the console's own `clear arp-cache`, which is not ARP logging.

STEP 0 -- baseline (feature OFF): a full ARP resolution must log nothing    done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The CLI surface first, read-only, with no command executed:

awplus(config)# arp log ?
  mac-address-format  MAC address format
  <cr>
awplus(config)# arp log mac-address-format ?
  ieee  Standard format for a MAC address

  Then the baseline:

awplus# show running-config | include arp
awplus#                                  <-- no arp line of any kind; `arp log` is not set
awplus# clear log buffered
awplus# clear arp-cache

  leg `L0-log-off`:
  sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L0-log-off --out <capture dir> \
    --iface eth3 --watch eth4 --probe 192.168.48.2 --sender-ip 192.168.10.2 \
    --sender-mac 00:11:22:33:44:01 --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 3 \
    --answer 192.168.48.2=00:11:22:33:44:02,192.168.10.2=00:11:22:33:44:01 \
    --answer-iface eth3,eth4 --wait 6

answerer sent 1 ARP reply/replies (eth3:0, eth4:1)
  x1  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.48.2 tell 192.168.48.1  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x1  ARP-REPLY 192.168.48.2 is-at 00:11:22:33:44:02    [00:11:22:33:44:02 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
                                         <-- a real resolution happened on the wire

awplus# show log
  (19 lines. The ONLY line matching "arp", case-insensitive, is the command audit echo
   of the console's own input:
     2026 Oct 15 05:42:44 user.notice awplus IMISH[2855]: [manager@ttyS0]clear arp-cache
   There is NO ARP_LOG line and no ARP event line of any kind.)

  => STEP 0 done: with the feature off, a complete ARP exchange -- the DUT's own
     broadcast request, the responder's reply, the entry created -- leaves nothing in
     the log. Every count in the steps below is measured against this zero, using the
     identical leg.

