--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# arp log
awplus(config)# end
awplus# show running-config | include arp
arp log
awplus# clear log buffered
awplus# clear arp-cache

  leg `L1-log-on`: the SAME arpleg.py invocation as STEP 0.
answerer sent 1 ARP reply/replies (eth3:0, eth4:1)
  x1  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.48.2 tell 192.168.48.1  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x1  ARP-REPLY 192.168.48.2 is-at 00:11:22:33:44:02    [00:11:22:33:44:02 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]

awplus# show log
2026 Oct 15 05:43:47 user.notice awplus HSL[936]: ARP_LOG port1.0.48 vlan48 del 0011.2233.4402 (192.168.48.2)
2026 Oct 15 05:43:55 user.notice awplus HSL[936]: ARP_LOG port1.0.48 vlan48 add 0011.2233.4402 (192.168.48.2)

  Identical crafted traffic: zero ARP_LOG lines with the feature off, two with it on.
  The log records BOTH directions of the cache change -- `del` at 05:43:47, the entry
  removed by `clear arp-cache`, and `add` at 05:43:55, the entry created by the
  responder's reply to the DUT's own request. Each line carries the egress port
  (port1.0.48), the VLAN (vlan48), the MAC in AW+ dotted form (0011.2233.4402) and the
  IP (192.168.48.2) -- all four matching the frames in the capture above. Facility and
  severity `user.notice`, emitting process `HSL[936]`.

  => STEP 1 PASS: `arp log` turns ARP cache events into log lines, and the lines are
     correct, not merely present -- every field in them is independently confirmed by
     the frames the same leg captured, and both the add and the delete direction are
     reported.

