answerer sent 1 ARP reply/replies (eth4:1)
  x1  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.48.2 tell 192.168.48.1  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x1  ARP-REPLY 192.168.48.2 is-at 00:11:22:33:44:02    [00:11:22:33:44:02 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]

awplus# show log
2026 Oct 15 05:49:56 user.notice awplus HSL[944]: ARP_LOG port1.0.48 vlan48 add 0011.2233.4402 (192.168.48.2)
                                         <-- HSL[944]: a NEW process id, not HSL[936]

  Only an `add` appears because the post-boot cache was already empty, so the
  `clear arp-cache` had nothing to delete -- consistent with the dynamic cache not
  surviving a reboot.

  => STEP 4 PASS: the setting is back in the configuration AND still working -- a
     crafted resolution after the reboot produced its ARP_LOG line, with the same port,
     VLAN, MAC and IP. The emitting process id changed from HSL[936] before the reload
     to HSL[944] after it, which is independent proof that the line comes from a freshly
     started process and the reboot was real, not a stale buffer re-read.
